{"name":"Anjou Pear","hex":"#cdca9f","fetched":"2026-10-11","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"pear","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["An edible fruit produced by the pear tree, similar to an apple but typically elongated towards the stem.","A type of fruit tree (Pyrus communis)."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/pear"}],"entries":[{"title":"D'Anjou","blocks":[{"text":"The d'Anjou pear, sometimes referred to as the Beurré d'Anjou or simply Anjou, is a short-necked cultivar of European pear. The variety was originally named 'Nec Plus Meuris' in Europe and the name 'Anjou' or 'd'Anjou' was erroneously applied to the variety when introduced to America and England. It is thought to have originated in the mid-19th century, in Belgium or France."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0b/D%27anjou_pear.jpg/330px-D%27anjou_pear.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/D'Anjou","wikidata":"Q2745252"},{"title":"Pear","blocks":[{"text":"Pears are a genus of trees, Pyrus, in the family Rosaceae, that bear an edible apple-like fruit of the same name. Several species of pears are cultivated for their fruit and juices, while others are grown as ornamental flowering trees."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/cf/Pears.jpg/330px-Pears.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pear","wikidata":"Q434"},{"title":"Shades of yellow","blocks":[{"text":"Varieties of the color yellow may differ in hue, chroma or lightness, or in two or three of these qualities. Variations in value are also called tints and shades, a tint being a yellow or other hue mixed with white, a shade being mixed with black. A large selection of these various colors is shown below."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/9e/Color_icon_yellow.svg/330px-Color_icon_yellow.svg.pn